# Supplemental PIV hardware probes

## Prepared scope

Seven audit-only cases address material gaps left by the canonical integration assertions.
Their unique assembly is `AuditPiv001.Hardware.IntegrationTests`, with a discoverable
project named `Yubico.YubiKit.AuditPiv001.Hardware.IntegrationTests.csproj` under
`../validation/hardware-probe/tests/.../src/`. Real project references supply PIV, Core,
Tests.Shared and the complete actual PivTool executable assembly. The probes call the
public SDK and real sample helper; they introduce no product implementation or assertion
changes.

The coordinator reported the version-2 cleanup control passed RESET, factory PIN and
AES192 management authentication, three fresh public captures and unchanged F9. That is
the prerequisite control for this packet, distinct from future supplemental results. The
canonical unattended batch is still coordinator-owned. This worker performs source
preparation only and opens no device.

Every case uses `[SkippableTheory]`, real `[WithYubiKey]`, a `Serial103Filter`, explicit
SmartCard selection and an additional live serial assertion. The coordinator keeps the
existing tracked shared allowlist temporarily at exactly `[103]`; the wrapper checks that
configuration before starting. Serial 125 remains outside mutation scope. The coordinator
preserves/restores the original tracked configuration under its existing assignment.

## Case matrix

| Case / filter suffix | Hypothesis and independent oracle | Persistent effects and restoration |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `P01_X25519_Rfc7748AliceAndBobAgree` | Import RFC7748 §6.1 Alice's scalar into 9D; metadata must report imported X25519 and the exact Alice public u-coordinate. CalculateSecret with Bob's raw public key must equal the RFC's 32-byte little-endian shared secret, compared in constant time. | Imports 9D with PinOnce/TouchNever; verifies factory PIN. Clears caller scalar, expected secret, copied disposable private key and returned mutable secret. Independent vendor cleanup follows process termination. |
| `P02_PinAlwaysConsumesVerification_PinOnceReusesIt` | Generate P256 9C/Always and 9A/Once. Verify PIN, host-verify first 9C signature, observe second unverified 9C operation, then reverify and host-verify the same key. Two 9A signatures follow one verification. Desired second-Always rejection stays asserted after same-key controls. | Creates 9C and 9A, supplies correct factory PIN only. All owned digests/signatures are cleared. No reset inside the test. |
| `P03_MoveAndDeletePreserveIndependentCertificates` | Generate 9A P256; store real software-key certificates in 9A and Retired1 whose SPKI deliberately differs from the slot key. Move key to Retired1, require exact moved metadata SPKI and host-verified signature, then delete it. Both certificates retain exact DER through move and delete. | Generates 9A, writes certificates 9A/82, moves/deletes the ordinary key. Software private key stays in a disposable host crypto object; no private file/export. Vendor cleanup clears residual ordinary certificate objects. |
| `P04_AttestationBindsGeneratedKeyToPreservedIssuer` | Generate P256 9A; parse complete attestation DER, verify TBSCertificate's SHA256/PKCS1 signature with captured F9 RSA public key, and compare attested/generated/metadata SPKI. Require unchanged issuer certificate bytes. | Generates 9A PinNever/TouchNever and attests it. F9 stays read-only. This proves generated-key/issuer binding and continued F9 private-key use, while manufacturer CA-chain trust remains outside the claim. |
| `P05_ActualPivToolRsaSignsOriginalMessage` | Generate RSA2048 9C. Independently construct EMSA-PKCS1-v1_5 SHA256 block, use raw SDK signing and host VerifyData as a control. Call actual `Signing.SignDataAsync` and require its output to host-verify SHA256/PKCS1 over the preserved original message. | Generates 9C/Once/Never and verifies correct PIN. Expected PIV-IMP-005 failure remains a failed assertion; wrapper still retains raw exit and performs vendor cleanup. |
| `P06_ActualPivToolEd25519SignsOriginalMessage` | Generate Ed25519 9C. Independent OpenSSL verifies a raw SDK message signature first. Actual sample helper output must verify over the original message. A separate verification against SHA256(message) identifies the suspected prehash behavior without weakening the original-message assertion. | Generates 9C/Once/Never. OpenSSL receives only public SPKI, known public message/digest fixtures and signatures. Expected PIV-IMP-005 failure remains visible. |
| `P07_CancelTouchWait_DrainThenFreshSameKeyRecovery` | Human leaves TouchAlways Retired20 untouched. Observe prompt, allow one second for the exchange to start, cancel, then require terminal task before disposal. Open a fresh session and human-touch the same key; compare metadata SPKI and host-verify the recovery signature before reset. Record callback/operation timestamps and actual terminal status. Desired cancellation and Cancelled callback assertions follow recovery. | Generates only Retired20 P256/Never/Always. `RequiresUserPresence` and `Slow` traits separate the ceremony. Pending/drain markers prevent automatic reset until same-key evidence is complete. |

P02 records the exception type and any unexpected signature's independent validity. The
current SDK maps raw `6982` to `InvalidOperationException` with the security-status message
in `PrivateKeyOperations.cs`; that mapped-status provenance is recorded explicitly. This
probe does not label the exception as an independently captured raw status word.

After P01's .NET process terminates, the wrapper runs one serial/reader/source-guarded
vendor reference on the same 9D key before cleanup. The installed Python public
`calculate_secret()` uses its own request/response encoding and cryptography's Bob public
key. Its metadata check requires the imported RFC Alice public value, then its agreement
must match the RFC secret. It records only public metadata and equality booleans. A .NET
mismatch with successful vendor agreement narrows the defect to the SDK path; two
mismatches retain firmware/reference-format questions. A missing imported fixture records
an unmet same-key precondition rather than inventing agreement evidence. The sidecar has
a 30-second external process bound; timeout holds cleanup for coordinator investigation.
Its Python immutable vendor intermediates have the same zeroization limitation as the
factory-default helper; owned mutable expected/result copies are cleared and no secret
or secret hash is logged. The inputs are published RFC fixtures, not production keys.

Each normal case has a 90-second cooperative token; sample cases use 120 seconds. P07 has
a 150-second envelope, 10 seconds for the request, 45 seconds for terminal drain and 60
seconds for fresh same-key recovery. The normal runner has a 180-second test-session
watchdog, while the outer hardware-bearing command uses a 300-second TERM/KILL bound.
RSA2048 generation is included in its explicit case budget. Native I/O may outlive a
cooperative token; the wrapper treats watchdog termination as an evidence boundary.

## Coordinator commands

Serialize these commands after the current canonical batch has fully terminated. Run
the build/test skills for coordinator execution as required by root instructions. The
prepared wrapper issues only the repository `dotnet toolchain.cs` build/test entry points.
From the repository root:

```bash
python docs/research/documentation-audit/modules/piv/pass-1/piv-001/validation/hardware-probe/run-probe.py P01_X25519_Rfc7748AliceAndBobAgree --name p01-x25519-v1
python docs/research/documentation-audit/modules/piv/pass-1/piv-001/validation/hardware-probe/run-probe.py P02_PinAlwaysConsumesVerification_PinOnceReusesIt --name p02-pin-policy-v1
python docs/research/documentation-audit/modules/piv/pass-1/piv-001/validation/hardware-probe/run-probe.py P03_MoveAndDeletePreserveIndependentCertificates --name p03-key-cert-v1
python docs/research/documentation-audit/modules/piv/pass-1/piv-001/validation/hardware-probe/run-probe.py P04_AttestationBindsGeneratedKeyToPreservedIssuer --name p04-attestation-v1
python docs/research/documentation-audit/modules/piv/pass-1/piv-001/validation/hardware-probe/run-probe.py P05_ActualPivToolRsaSignsOriginalMessage --name p05-tool-rsa-v1
python docs/research/documentation-audit/modules/piv/pass-1/piv-001/validation/hardware-probe/run-probe.py P06_ActualPivToolEd25519SignsOriginalMessage --name p06-tool-ed25519-v1
```

Run the human ceremony separately when the user can first withhold touch and then supply
the requested fresh-recovery touch:

```bash
python docs/research/documentation-audit/modules/piv/pass-1/piv-001/validation/hardware-probe/run-probe.py P07_CancelTouchWait_DrainThenFreshSameKeyRecovery --name p07-cancellation-v1 --human-coordinated
```

Each invocation creates a new exclusive `hardware-probe/evidence/<name>/` directory.
The wrapper clean-builds only the unique audit project through toolchain, immediately
retains actual Csc context, runs independent vendor-v2 restoration before the case, runs
exactly the named method through toolchain and retains its raw exit/RESULT logs, then runs
independent vendor-v2 cleanup after the runner has terminated—even after ordinary assertion
failure. Vendor cleanup retains its own before/after/auth readbacks. P05/P06 intentionally
return failure if the desired original-message contract fails; `expected_failure` in the
receipt describes the hypothesis and never converts failure into success.

The wrapper retains build/test logs before toolchain rotation, exact cleanup receipts,
tool source identities, all compiled DLL hashes before and after the test, and loaded host,
PIV, Tests.Shared and PivTool DLL path/hash events. Different binaries after an incremental
test build invalidate the original compiler binding and require a new versioned capture.
The build/compiler record remains retained so a failed membership assumption can be
corrected narrowly without erasing its original output.

The audit filename also matches the toolchain's broad `--project Piv` substring selector.
For subsequent canonical-only runs, use `--project Yubico.YubiKit.Piv.IntegrationTests`.
The current already-started batch remains coordinator-owned. Direct audit execution
without the wrapper's evidence-directory environment fails before opening a PIV session.

## Compiler and runtime provenance

`Directory.Build.props` imports the repository's real props, keeps net10/C#14, nullable,
warnings-as-errors and analyzers, and isolates this host's obj/bin under
`/tmp/opencode/piv-001-hardware`. The project filename contains the repository prefix and
its path contains both `tests` and `src`, matching the current toolchain's actual project
discovery. Its assembly/root namespace are unique. No root solution or product project
is edited to admit the audit host.

The local targets preserve actual `CscCommandLineArgs`, Compile paths, resolved reference
assemblies, analyzers, analyzer configuration and final policy immediately after the clean
build. `capture-compiler.py` requires actual PivTool reference membership, strict policy
flags and hashes all listed dependencies. It separately records all current PivTool source
identities; the real helper remains in the project reference rather than a copied stand-in.
The capture pattern is adapted from the corrected OpenPGP hardware host; the original
OpenPGP archive remains untouched. `preparation.md` records provenance and preparation
checks. The coordinator must inspect the full actual-Csc reference/analyzer argument lists
and zero-warning RESULT, rather than treating capture creation as a complete compilation
verdict.

The wrapper records the OpenSSL executable hash and `version -a` output. P06's three
verification child processes each have a 10-second budget, drain redirected streams,
kill/await the child on timeout and retain public fixtures for independent replay. Missing
OpenSSL or an unsupported provider is an oracle prerequisite failure, distinct from an SDK
signature mismatch. .NET's host crypto performs P256 and RSA verification independently
from the device operation. RFC7748 §6.1 supplies the published X25519 byte-level oracle.

## Drain and preservation boundaries

P07 writes `recovery-pending.txt` before starting the private-key operation. The original
task runs on a worker so the caller can request cancellation even if native exchange entry
blocks synchronously. A watchdog writes `drain-blocked.txt` and exits the host if request,
drain or recovery exceeds its bound. This avoids concurrent session disposal or RESET
against an active exchange. The runner's exit alone supplies no native-cancellation
guarantee. The wrapper holds cleanup when a pending/drain marker remains or its outer
timeout fired. The coordinator preserves that state and establishes terminal/drain and
same-key recovery evidence before any subsequent reset decision.

After terminal operation observation, the old session/connection disposes before a fresh
session opens. Only successful fresh-session host verification and disposal rename the
pending marker to `recovery-completed.txt`. An unexpected completion/error still receives
the recovery control before the desired cancellation assertion. An early setup/assertion
failure after the pending marker conservatively holds cleanup. This is a material evidence
boundary rather than an automatic destructive recovery path.

F9 receives public certificate/metadata reads and its normal attestation signing role
only. Every generation/import/move/delete/write targets ordinary slots. The suite sets
reversible Never/Once/Always policies and configures no F9 policy, Bio enrollment or fixed
policy. Existing successful touch coverage can remain a separate canonical ceremony;
cached touch is outside these seven bounded supplemental cases.

All credential clones and owned secret/private-input/output buffers clear in `finally` or
their private-key disposer. Probe code borrows caller views without casting them writable.
The actual sample exposes `SigningResult.Signature` as read-only memory. Its caller can
dispose references but has no public zeroing owner; this probe records that limitation and
leaves the sample-owned backing buffer untouched. Public signature/fixture files contain
no private scalars, management key, PIN, protected management secret or production data.

## Result rows and current status

The prepared denominator is **seven source methods**, each with one serial-filtered theory
row when prerequisites bind. Compilation, discovery, execution, assertions, independent
cleanup and platform qualification are separate result fields. All seven supplemental
cases are currently **prepared / not executed by this worker**. Populate new execution
rows from the retained runner and receipt evidence; prior PR745, canonical batch and cleanup
control results keep their original scopes and counts. Linux USB development-firmware
results will leave NFC, older releases, other operating systems, Bio and AOT qualification
outstanding.
